1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,312
This is ECE 358, video 10 b. The topic is TCP flow control and connection management. Video 10 a covered segments, ACKs, and retransmission.

2
00:00:13,762 --> 00:00:36,354
After this video, you can explain why TCP needs flow control, and calculate r wind. You can trace the in-flight data against r wind over time. You can write the 3-way handshake and the close. At the end, we solve question bank questions 36 and 40 to 42, and problem 5 of problem set 3.

3
00:00:36,804 --> 00:00:44,868
Slides 63 to 66 give the motivation for flow control. This is the receiver protocol stack.

4
00:00:45,318 --> 00:00:53,787
Data from the sender comes up through the I P code and the TCP code, into the TCP socket receiver buffers.

5
00:00:54,237 --> 00:00:58,675
The application process removes, or reads, data from the top.

6
00:00:59,125 --> 00:01:04,522
What happens if the network layer delivers data faster than the application reads it?

7
00:01:04,972 --> 00:01:08,855
The buffer fills up. Data enters fast and leaves slowly.

8
00:01:09,305 --> 00:01:16,217
When the buffer is full, it has no space for new data. New data is lost. This is buffer overflow.

9
00:01:16,667 --> 00:01:26,736
Flow control solves this. The receiver gives feedback to the sender, to control its data rate. So the sender does not overflow the receiver's buffer.

10
00:01:27,186 --> 00:01:35,613
The tool is the receive window field in the TCP header. It gives the number of bytes that the receiver is willing to accept.

11
00:01:36,063 --> 00:01:42,655
Slide 67 gives the mechanism. The receive buffer, RcvBuffer, has a fixed size.

12
00:01:43,105 --> 00:01:53,067
Socket options set this size. The typical default is 4096 bytes. Many operating systems adjust it automatically.

13
00:01:53,517 --> 00:02:00,707
Part of the buffer holds data that the application has not read yet. Here, 1500 bytes.

14
00:02:01,157 --> 00:02:05,487
The rest is free buffer space. This free space is r wind.

15
00:02:05,937 --> 00:02:17,436
So r wind equals RcvBuffer minus the buffered data. 4096 minus 1500 is 2596 bytes.

16
00:02:17,886 --> 00:02:28,681
The receiver advertises r wind. It writes the value in the receive window field of the TCP header, in every segment that it sends to the other side.

17
00:02:29,131 --> 00:02:39,712
The sender limits the amount of unACKed, or in-flight, data to the r wind that it received: 2596 bytes.

18
00:02:40,162 --> 00:02:42,594
Here, 1000 bytes are in flight.

19
00:02:43,044 --> 00:02:57,615
2596 minus 1000 is 1596. So the sender can send only 1596 more bytes. Then it must wait for an ACK.

20
00:02:58,065 --> 00:03:05,617
The in-flight data is never more than the free space. This guarantees that the receive buffer does not overflow.

21
00:03:06,067 --> 00:03:19,421
Here is flow control over time. The numbers come from the handshake on slide 70. The server has a receive buffer of 5000 bytes. So the client got r wind 5000.

22
00:03:19,871 --> 00:03:23,157
The client data starts at byte 8001.

23
00:03:23,607 --> 00:03:33,953
The client sends segment A: sequence number 8001, 2500 bytes. Now 2500 bytes are in flight.

24
00:03:34,403 --> 00:03:47,203
The client sends segment B, another 2500 bytes. Its sequence number is 8001 plus 2500, which is 10501.

25
00:03:47,653 --> 00:03:58,555
Now 2500 plus 2500, which is 5000 bytes, are in flight. This equals r wind, so the client must stop.

26
00:03:59,005 --> 00:04:04,679
At the server, segment A arrives. The buffer holds 2500 bytes.

27
00:04:05,129 --> 00:04:14,089
The application reads 1000 bytes. 2500 minus 1000 leaves 1500 bytes.

28
00:04:14,539 --> 00:04:22,902
Then segment B arrives. 1500 plus 2500 is 4000 bytes in the buffer.

29
00:04:23,352 --> 00:04:32,440
The server sends one cumulative ACK: 10501 plus 2500, which is 13001.

30
00:04:32,890 --> 00:04:38,159
Its new r wind is 5000 minus 4000, which is 1000.

31
00:04:38,609 --> 00:04:48,145
When the ACK arrives, all bytes are ACKed. The client has 0 bytes in flight. So it may send r wind, 1000 bytes.

32
00:04:48,595 --> 00:04:58,814
It sends segment C: sequence number 13001, 1000 bytes. Then it stops again, because 1000 equals r wind.

33
00:04:59,264 --> 00:05:08,565
When C arrives, the buffer holds 4000 plus 1000, which is 5000 bytes. It is full, but no byte is lost.

34
00:05:09,015 --> 00:05:17,122
Slide 69 names four header fields that open a connection. First, the SYN flag. It requests a connection.

35
00:05:17,572 --> 00:05:23,695
Second, the initial sequence number, ISN. It is the first sequence number of a side.

36
00:05:24,145 --> 00:05:25,382
Third, the ACK.

37
00:05:25,832 --> 00:05:28,520
Fourth, the initial receive window size.

38
00:05:28,970 --> 00:05:42,175
Why is the ISN needed? In the early days, a connection closed, and a new connection opened right away between the same two end points. The new connection numbered its first data byte 0.

39
00:05:42,625 --> 00:05:53,143
A late segment of the old connection, for example sequence number 1001, then has a valid number. It is inside the range of the new connection.

40
00:05:53,593 --> 00:05:57,305
So the new connection accepts old data as new data.

41
00:05:57,755 --> 00:06:06,437
The solution of slide 69: the first data byte is not numbered 0. The ISN is randomly generated.

42
00:06:06,887 --> 00:06:16,445
For example, with ISN 15000, the new connection expects bytes from 15001. That is the ISN plus 1.

43
00:06:16,895 --> 00:06:22,591
The late segment 1001 is outside this range. So it is not accepted.

44
00:06:23,041 --> 00:06:30,187
Slide 70 shows the 3-way handshake. The server does a passive open: it calls listen, and waits.

45
00:06:30,637 --> 00:06:33,965
The client does an active open: it calls connect.

46
00:06:34,415 --> 00:06:37,765
The client picks a random ISN, 8000.

47
00:06:38,215 --> 00:06:42,439
Segment 1 has sequence number 8000 and the SYN flag.

48
00:06:42,889 --> 00:06:46,771
The server picks its own random ISN, 15000.

49
00:06:47,221 --> 00:06:54,539
Segment 2 has sequence number 15000, ACK number 8001, and the SYN and ACK flags.

50
00:06:54,989 --> 00:07:04,290
8001 is the client ISN plus 1: 8000 plus 1. It is the first data byte that the server expects.

51
00:07:04,740 --> 00:07:09,220
R wind is 5000, the size of the server's receive buffer.

52
00:07:09,670 --> 00:07:12,337
The connection is now open at the client.

53
00:07:12,787 --> 00:07:23,411
Segment 3 has sequence number 8001 and ACK number 15001. 15001 is the server ISN plus 1.

54
00:07:23,861 --> 00:07:28,661
Its r wind is 10,000, the size of the client's receive buffer.

55
00:07:29,111 --> 00:07:36,961
When segment 3 arrives, the connection is open at the server too. Now data transfer starts, in both directions.

56
00:07:37,411 --> 00:07:46,883
Slide 71 lists the contents. The SYN segment: SYN is 1, a random ISN, r wind undefined, and options.

57
00:07:47,333 --> 00:07:58,619
The SYN plus ACK segment: SYN is 1, a random ISN, and ACK is 1. The ACK number is the first data byte to be received. It carries r wind.

58
00:07:59,069 --> 00:08:03,293
The last ACK acknowledges the second SYN, and carries r wind.

59
00:08:03,743 --> 00:08:10,804
Slide 72 explains the close. Each side closes its own side of the connection when it needs to.

60
00:08:11,254 --> 00:08:21,003
It sends a segment with the FIN bit set to 1. FIN means: the sender of FIN has no more data to send. Here, the client sends FIN.

61
00:08:21,453 --> 00:08:24,312
The other side responds to the FIN with an ACK.

62
00:08:24,762 --> 00:08:33,061
The server still has data, so it sends this data. The client can still receive, because only the client side is closed.

63
00:08:33,511 --> 00:08:37,265
When the server has no more data, it sends its own FIN.

64
00:08:37,715 --> 00:08:43,881
The client responds with an ACK. Both FINs are ACKed, so the connection is closed.

65
00:08:44,331 --> 00:08:50,261
If the server has no more data when the FIN arrives, it combines its ACK with its own FIN.

66
00:08:50,711 --> 00:08:55,447
Then three segments close the connection: FIN, FIN plus ACK, and ACK.

67
00:08:55,897 --> 00:09:04,985
Both sides can also send FIN at the same time. TCP can handle this case too: each side ACKs the FIN that it receives.

68
00:09:05,435 --> 00:09:13,862
Now the question bank. Question 36 asks you to explain eight features of TCP. Pause the video and try it.

69
00:09:14,312 --> 00:09:18,963
a: point-to-point means one sender and one receiver per connection.

70
00:09:19,413 --> 00:09:28,501
b: reliable byte stream means that TCP numbers each byte, and delivers all bytes in order, with no message boundaries.

71
00:09:28,951 --> 00:09:33,068
c: full duplex means data in both directions in one connection.

72
00:09:33,518 --> 00:09:39,022
d: cumulative ACK means that ACK n confirms all bytes up to n minus 1.

73
00:09:39,472 --> 00:09:48,219
e: pipelining means many unACKed segments at the same time. Flow control and congestion control set the window.

74
00:09:48,669 --> 00:09:57,373
f: connection oriented means that a handshake sets the state before the data: window sizes, MSS, and ISN.

75
00:09:57,823 --> 00:10:02,580
g: flow controlled means that the sender does not overwhelm the receiver.

76
00:10:03,030 --> 00:10:10,049
h: congestion controlled means that the sender tries not to overwhelm the routers and links of the network.

77
00:10:10,499 --> 00:10:18,179
Question 40 asks for the purpose and the mechanism of TCP flow control. Pause the video and try it.

78
00:10:18,629 --> 00:10:26,863
The purpose. The network layer can deliver data faster than the application reads it. Then the receive buffer overflows.

79
00:10:27,313 --> 00:10:31,495
Flow control keeps the sender from overflowing the receiver's buffer.

80
00:10:31,945 --> 00:10:49,587
The mechanism has two sides. The receiver calculates r wind as RcvBuffer minus the buffered data. With the numbers of slide 67, 4096 minus 1500 is 2596.

81
00:10:50,037 --> 00:10:55,264
The receiver advertises r wind in the receive window field of its segments.

82
00:10:55,714 --> 00:11:03,650
The sender keeps its in-flight data at or below r wind: at most 2596 bytes.

83
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:05,977
So the buffer cannot overflow.

84
00:11:06,427 --> 00:11:18,203
Question 41 asks for two parameters exchanged at connection set-up. Question 42 asks how the FIN bit closes a connection. Pause the video and try them.

85
00:11:18,653 --> 00:11:31,112
Question 41. Each side sends its initial sequence number. On slide 70, the client ISN is 8000, and the server ISN is 15000.

86
00:11:31,562 --> 00:11:39,221
Each side also sends its receive window size: 5000 for the server, and 10,000 for the client.

87
00:11:39,671 --> 00:11:43,532
The SYN can also carry options, such as the MSS.

88
00:11:43,982 --> 00:11:50,190
Question 42. A side with no more data sends a segment with FIN equal to 1.

89
00:11:50,640 --> 00:11:57,851
The other side ACKs it. If the other side also has no more data, it can combine the ACK with its own FIN.

90
00:11:58,301 --> 00:12:04,722
Otherwise, the other side sends its data first, and then its FIN. The first side ACKs this FIN.

91
00:12:05,172 --> 00:12:08,969
When both FINs are ACKed, the connection is closed.

92
00:12:09,419 --> 00:12:37,707
Problem 5 of problem set 3. Host A sends an enormous file to host B over TCP. There is no loss, and the timers never expire. The link of host A has rate R. The process can write into its socket at S, which is 10 R. The receive buffer holds the entire file. The send buffer holds only 1 percent of the file. What stops the process from writing at rate S all the time? Pause the video and try it.

93
00:12:38,157 --> 00:12:46,861
Is it flow control? No. The receive buffer can hold the whole file. So it never fills, and r wind never stops the sender.

94
00:12:47,311 --> 00:12:55,845
Is it congestion control? No. There is no loss and no timeout. So congestion control does not throttle the sender.

95
00:12:56,295 --> 00:13:02,076
It is something else: the send buffer. Data enters it at rate S, which is 10 R.

96
00:13:02,526 --> 00:13:05,427
Data leaves it at most at the link rate R.

97
00:13:05,877 --> 00:13:12,256
So the send buffer fills at S minus R. That is 10 R minus R, which is 9 R.

98
00:13:12,706 --> 00:13:26,231
Call the file size F. The buffer holds 1 percent of F. So it is full after point 0 1 F over 9 R, which is F over 900 R seconds. That is very soon.

99
00:13:26,681 --> 00:13:32,825
After that, the process can write only as fast as TCP sends data out: rate R.

100
00:13:33,275 --> 00:13:41,339
So the process passes data at an average rate of R, much less than S. This matches the problem set solution.

101
00:13:41,789 --> 00:13:49,789
Here is the recap. One. Flow control: the receiver controls the sender, so the receive buffer does not overflow.

102
00:13:50,239 --> 00:14:02,933
Two. R wind is RcvBuffer minus the buffered data. The receiver advertises it in the receive window field. The sender keeps its in-flight data at or below r wind.

103
00:14:03,383 --> 00:14:09,740
Three. The ISN is random, so data of an old connection is not accepted in a new one.

104
00:14:10,190 --> 00:14:20,750
Four. The handshake: SYN, then SYN plus ACK, then ACK. Each ACK number is the other ISN plus 1. Each side sends its receive window.

105
00:14:21,200 --> 00:14:33,829
Five. Each side closes its own side with a FIN, and the other side ACKs it. Video 11 covers TCP congestion control. That is the end of video 10 b.
